            <ArticleTitle>Women's Experiences of Undergoing Total Knee Joint Replacement Surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>86-95</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1089-9472(16)00094-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jopan.2015.11.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of the study was to describe women's experiences of undergoing total knee joint replacement surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A qualitative approach was used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A content analysis of the text from interviews with five women was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The time before surgery was marked by the experience of constant pain, which affected the women negatively in their everyday lives. During surgery, the information provided by the staff gave each woman a sense of security; the women handed over responsibility to the staff and experienced a sensation of relief. The postoperative period was characterized by a feeling of joy when the surgery was over, although a rough and tedious rehabilitation phase then began. Challenges in everyday life were a factor for motivation and confidence, although postoperative pain was experienced as discouraging.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Support from health care staff is an important factor for coping with everyday life during the preoperative, perioperative and postoperative phases of undergoing knee joint replacement.</AbstractText>
